<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warsaw</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Warsaw is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> River. It became the royal residence and effective capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> moved the court there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> around 1596, and from 1569 it was the regular seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and of royal elections. Accounts of the Commonwealth discuss it in the context of the state's foundation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warsaw.jpg" alt="Warsaw" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emptywords · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleja_Niepdleglosci_Warsaw_2022_aerial_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Warsaw lies in east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, in the heartland of the Masovian Plain, about 260 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and 523 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. The name in Polish is Warszawa, and its origin is uncertain. One hypothesis reads it as belonging to Warsz, a short form of the Old Polish name Warcisław, and the original Warszawa was a small fishing settlement on the river bank. Folk etymology tells of the fisherman Wars and the mermaid Sawa, who lived in the Vistula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first fortified settlements on the site were at Bródno in the 9th or 10th century and at Jazdów in the 12th or 13th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-of-masovia">Bolesław II of Masovia</a>, Prince of Płock, established the town in about 1300, and in 1413 it was officially made capital of the Masovian Duchy, when it had about 4,500 inhabitants. The city rose to prominence after 1596, when Sigismund III moved the Polish capital and royal court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. By the 18th century Warsaw had passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> as the most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> until 1795 and afterwards the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Duchy of Warsaw.</p><p>The 19th century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> made it one of the largest and most densely populated cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it held the world's second-largest Jewish population after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, about 30 percent of its people in the late 1930s. The city was bombed and besieged in 1939. Much of the historic city was destroyed and its diverse population decimated by the Ghetto Uprising of 1943, the Warsaw Uprising of 1944 and systematic razing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Warsaw is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with about 1.87 million residents, and the seat of both houses of the Polish Parliament. The reconstructed Old Town was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1980. Other landmarks are the Royal Castle, Sigismund's Column and Wilanów Palace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warsaw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1807 Napoleon created the Duchy of <b>Warsaw</b> as a French client state from land taken from Prussia, and enlarged it at Austria's expense after the Battle of Wagram in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Agreements with Sigismund III in 1605 and the Treaty of <b>Warsaw</b> of 1611 confirmed Brandenburg's claim, in return guaranteeing Catholic worship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…a Polish mission led by Kazimierz Michałowski before the area was flooded by Lake Nasser in the 1960s, are now in the National Museum in <b>Warsaw</b> and the Sudan National Museum in Khartoum and show bishops, kings and saints in a style that blends Byzantine and local traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was an elective monarchy in which the king was chosen by the entire szlachta nobility in mass elections, usually held at Wola outside <b>Warsaw</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Neighbouring cities of the new republic had belonged to different empires, so there was no direct railway connection between <b>Warsaw</b> and Kraków until 1934, and the country began with five different currencies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In Poland, when the Jagiellonian line ended with the death of Sigismund II Augustus in 1572, the nobles decided that every nobleman who came to the election field near <b>Warsaw</b> should vote.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
